<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Can I Have a Pencil?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for simple tasks. Interactive: Write a simple collaborative text with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple, clear instructions for a game in a polite manner (e.g., writing, "Please wait for your turn. Then roll the dice."). Can co-write short dialogues with peers using polite requests (e.g., writing, "I have a pencil, but I don't have an eraser. Can I borrow your eraser?" "Good idea. Your turn now."). Can write a short note to explain their inability to complete a task using polite expressions (e.g., writing, "I'm sorry. I can't roll the dice. Can you help me?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of school supplies (e.g., a pencil, paper, a bag, a desk). Students identify the items orally. Then, the teacher asks students to write the name of each item as it is shown. For example, "What is this?" (pencil). "Write 'pencil' on your mini-whiteboard/paper." This activates prior vocabulary and immediately engages them in writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing the vocabulary words clearly on the board, demonstrating correct letter formation for "pencil," "paper," "desk," "bag," "friend." The teacher then models making a polite request orally: "Can I have a pencil, please?" emphasizing the /p/ sound in "please" and the polite tone. The teacher writes this sentence on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "When do we say 'please'?" (Expected answer: "When we ask for something." or "To be polite.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "If I say 'Can I have your pencil, please?' am I being kind or not kind?" (Expected answer: "Kind.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue written on a large flashcard or projected, featuring two friends making polite requests for school supplies. The dialogue uses "Can I have...please?", "I have a...", "I don't have...", and "I can't...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Dialogue:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Friend 1: "Hi, John! I need a pencil. Can I borrow your pencil, please?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Friend 2: "Sure! Here you are. I have a pencil."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Friend 1: "Thank you! Oh, I can't find my paper. Can I have some paper, please?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Friend 2: "I'm sorry, I don't have paper. Maybe ask Maria?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> "Writes simple, short notes or messages related to everyday needs and experiences, demonstrating appropriate use of politeness markers."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, pointing to the words. Students listen and follow along.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the dialogue silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks true/false questions: "Friend 1 needs a pen. True or False?" "Friend 2 has paper. True or False?" Students write "T" or "F" on their whiteboards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matching activity: Students draw lines to match sentences to the correct speaker (Friend 1 or Friend 2) based on the dialogue. (e.g., "Can I borrow your pencil?" -> Friend 1).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing polite requests using "Can I have...please?", "I can't", and present simple sentences (e.g., "I have a pencil, but I don't have an eraser.") with target vocabulary (pencil, paper, desk, friend, bag, help, borrow, ask, polite, kind).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Fill-in-the-blanks):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students complete them using words from a word bank (e.g., "can," "please," "have," "I," "can't").
                        <br>Example: "___ I ___ a ___ , ___ ?" (Can I have a pencil, please?)
                        <br>Example: "I'm sorry. I ___ help you." (I can't help you.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Builders):</b> The teacher displays picture prompts (e.g., a student needing an eraser, a student asking for help). Students write a polite request or statement for each picture using sentence frames: "Can I ______ ______ , please?" or "I _______ _______ an _______."
                        <br>Example: Picture of a student with no eraser -> Student writes: "Can I borrow your eraser, please?" or "I don't have an eraser."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Writing (Pair Prompts):</b> In pairs, students receive a set of prompts on cards (e.g., "Ask for a pencil," "Say you have paper," "Say you can't help"). They take turns writing a short two-line dialogue for each prompt on a shared piece of paper, focusing on polite expressions and correct grammar.
                        <br>Example Prompt: "Ask for a pencil. Friend says yes."
                        <br>Student A writes: "Can I have a pencil, please?"
                        <br>Student B writes: "Yes, you can. Here you are."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Writing of "Classroom Game Instructions" or a "Polite Request Note." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group will choose one of two writing tasks:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Option 1: Write Simple Instructions for a Game (e.g., "Pass the Item"):</b> Groups write 3-4 simple, clear instructions for a classroom game involving polite requests. Instructions must use polite expressions and target vocabulary/grammar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Instructions:</p>
                <ol style="margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Please wait for your turn.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask your friend, "Can I have the pencil, please?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When you get it, say "Thank you!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then pass it to the next friend.</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Option 2: Co-write a Short Dialogue for a "Lost Item" Scenario:</b> Groups create a 4-6 line dialogue where one student has lost an item (e.g., a pencil) and politely asks several friends for help, including expressing inability ("I can't find it") and making polite requests to borrow ("Can I borrow...?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Organize students into groups.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provide large sheets of paper or poster board for writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Circulate, offering assistance with vocabulary and sentence structure, guiding students to use the target grammar and expressions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage peer collaboration and checking each other's writing for clarity and politeness.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist while groups are writing and reviews their final written products. The checklist focuses on the accurate use of target grammar, vocabulary, and polite expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their collaboratively written game instructions or dialogue. The teacher collects and briefly reviews them for completion and accuracy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of "Can I have...please?" and "I can't."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of target vocabulary (pencil, paper, friend, borrow, help, wait, thank you, please).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and comprehensibility of instructions/dialogue.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstration of collaborative writing (e.g., shared ideas, contributions from all members).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following questions on a small slip of paper to hand in:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did I use 'please' and 'thank you' in my group's writing? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word I wrote today?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy about writing today? What was difficult?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Why is it important to write politely?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the writing activities? (e.g., collaborative spirit, sentence formation, use of target vocabulary like 'kind' or 'helpful').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to improve their writing accuracy and fluency, particularly with "I can't" and the /k/ sound? (e.g., more sentence frames, explicit spelling practice for specific words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better connect the pronunciation focus (/p/ in 'please', /k/ in 'can't', blending in 'kind') to writing activities in future lessons?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their collaboratively written dialogues or instructions to present to the class, potentially adapting them into a short role-play or explaining the game rules, further integrating their writing skills with oral communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>